This is the help documentation for the govax VAX emulator.  Topic sections are
marked by a "$" delimiter.  This is followed by as many tokens of the help
command as were parsed, separated by commas.  Note that if the token is 
less than four characters long, it must be blank padded.  For example, 
"HELP SET TRACE" would resolve to locating the marker "$SET ,TRAC".  The 
"$" must be the first character of the line, and the line can only contain 
the keyword match string.  The text is displayed until the next "$" marker 
is read, or the end of the file is found.

The only exception is if you have more than one possible key tag in a row 
for the same block of text.  For example, "HELP RUN" and "HELP GO" might 
both be tags for the same command if "GO" is a synonym for "RUN". 

$SHOW,LOGI
$SH  ,LOGI
$SHOW,LOG 
$SH  ,LOG 
    The SHOW LOGICAL command displays logical names and their equivalence
    strings, in the same format as VMS.

    Syntax:

         SHOW LOGICAL [logical-name[,...]]

    Qualifiers:

        /PROCESS, /JOB, /GROUP, /SYSTEM, /TABLE=(table[,...])

            Search only the named table(s).  By default the tables that
            LNM$DCL_LOGICAL names are searched: the process, job, group,
            and system tables.

        /FULL

            Also show each name's access mode, such as [super] or [exec],
            and each equivalence string's translation attributes
            ([concealed], [terminal]).

        /STRUCTURE

            Show the logical name directories and the tables in each.

    With no logical name, every name in each table searched is listed
    under the table's name.  A logical name containing the * or %
    wildcards lists the matching names the same way.  Any other logical
    name is shown from the first table that has it, followed by the
    translation of each equivalence string that is itself a logical name,
    numbered by level:

         $ SHOW LOGICAL MYDISK
           "MYDISK" = "WORK4" (LNM$PROCESS_TABLE)
         1 "WORK4" = "$255$DUA17:" (LNM$SYSTEM_TABLE)

$SHOW,MEMO
$SHOW,MEM
$SH  ,MEMO
$SH  ,MEM
    The SHOW MEMORY command displays the amount of physical and virtual
    memory available on the virtual VAX. With paging on (VMINIT/PAGING),
    it also shows how many page frames are on the free list, on the
    modified list, and in use, by the kind of page each holds (a process's,
    a global section's, or the system's).

$SHOW,TRAN
$SH  ,TRAN
    The SHOW TRANSLATION command displays one level of translation of a
    logical name: the first match found, with no further translation.

    Syntax:

         SHOW TRANSLATION [/TABLE=table] logical-name

    Qualifier:

        /TABLE=table

            The table to search, or a logical name that translates to a
            list of tables.  The default is LNM$DCL_LOGICAL (the process,
            group, and system tables).

$DEFI
$DEF 
    The DEFINE command creates a logical name, or replaces an existing
    one defined in the same table at the same access mode.

    Syntax:

         DEFINE logical-name equivalence-string[,...]

    More than one equivalence string makes the name a search list.  Any
    trailing colon is kept as part of the logical name.  Unquoted text is
    converted to uppercase.

    Qualifiers:

        /PROCESS, /JOB, /GROUP, /SYSTEM, /TABLE=table

            The table to define the name in.  The default is the process
            table, LNM$PROCESS.  The job table, LNM$JOB, is shared by the
            console's process and every subprocess it creates.  /TABLE accepts a table name or a logical
            name that translates to one, such as LNM$PROCESS.

        /USER_MODE, /SUPERVISOR_MODE, /EXECUTIVE_MODE

            The access mode of the name.  The default is supervisor mode.
            A user-mode name in the process table is deleted when the
            next program started with RUN exits.

        /TRANSLATION_ATTRIBUTES=(CONCEALED,TERMINAL)

            CONCEALED shows the logical name in place of the device it
            stands for.  TERMINAL stops iterative translation at this
            name.  The attributes apply to every equivalence string.

        /LOG (default), /NOLOG

            Report when an existing definition is superseded.

    DEFINE/DEVICE, which defines a pseudodevice, is described separately.

$ASM 
$ASSE
    The ASM command assembles VAX source into memory, either from a file
    (ASM file) or, with no file, one statement at a time.  The console accepts
    it only until the microkernel is in place, which is how VAX.INIT loads
    it.  After that ASM is a debugger command: enter the debugger with DEBUG
    and type ASM there.  HELP ASM at the DBG> prompt describes the assembler.

$ANAL

    The ANALYZE/OBJECT command describes each record of an object file
    (or of an object library's modules) as VMS's ANALYZE/OBJECT does,
    in the same words and page layout: headers, global symbol directory
    subrecords with their flags, each TIR command with the linker's
    stack depth, STORE IMMEDIATE data as hex and characters, and the
    end of module record.  Errors in the file are flagged with "***"
    where they're found, and counted in a summary at the end.

    Syntax:

        ANALYZE/OBJECT <file>[,...][/HOST] [qualifiers...]

    Parameters:

        <file>          An object file (default type .OBJ), or an object
                        library (.OLB), whose modules are analyzed one
                        after another.  It can be a host file or a file
                        on a mounted volume, by the rules MACRO uses for
                        its source (see HELP MACRO).  A bare name is
                        found beside the file before it.

    Qualifiers:

        /OUTPUT[=<file>]
                        Writes the report to a file instead of the
                        console: by default the first file's name with
                        the type .ANL, beside it.

        /MHD, /GSD, /TIR, /TBT, /DBG, /LNK, /EOM
                        Shows only these kinds of records (/MHD shows
                        every header record).  Every record is still
                        checked and counted in the summary.

        /INCLUDE[=(<module>,...)]
                        Analyzes these modules of an object library;
                        names may hold the wildcards * and %.  Without
                        names, or without /INCLUDE, every module.

    Examples:

        ANALYZE/OBJECT "hello.obj"
        ANALYZE/OBJECT/OUTPUT DUA0:[000000]HELLO
        ANALYZE/OBJECT/GSD/INCLUDE=(MAIN) DUA0:[000000]MINE.OLB

    The ANALYZE/IMAGE command describes an executable or shareable image
    as VMS's ANALYZE/IMAGE does, in the same words and page layout: the
    image header (transfer addresses, symbol table locations, the
    image's name, ident, and link time, and each image section
    descriptor with its flags), then the image activator's fixup
    section (the shareable images the image uses, its G^ and .ADDRESS
    references to them, and the protection changes).  Errors in the
    file are flagged with "***" where they're found and counted at the
    end.

    Syntax:

        ANALYZE/IMAGE <file>[,...][/HOST] [qualifiers...]

    Parameters:

        <file>          An image file (default type .EXE), on the host
                        or on a mounted volume, found as for
                        ANALYZE/OBJECT.

    Qualifiers:

        /OUTPUT[=<file>]
                        Writes the report to a file instead of the
                        console: by default the first file's name with
                        the type .ANI, beside it.

        /HEADER         Shows only the image header.

        /FIXUP_SECTION  Shows the fixup section with the header (the
                        default when neither qualifier is given).

    Examples:

        ANALYZE/IMAGE "prog.exe"
        ANALYZE/IMAGE/HEADER/OUTPUT DUA0:[000000]PROG

$ASSI
    The ASSIGN command creates a logical name, like DEFINE, but with its
    parameters in the opposite order.  One trailing colon is removed from
    the logical name.

    Syntax:

         ASSIGN equivalence-string[,...] logical-name

    ASSIGN takes the same qualifiers as DEFINE.

$DEAS
    The DEASSIGN command deletes a logical name.  One trailing colon is
    removed from the logical name, so to delete a name that ends in a
    colon, give two.

    Syntax:

         DEASSIGN logical-name
         DEASSIGN/ALL

    Qualifiers:

        /PROCESS, /JOB, /GROUP, /SYSTEM, /TABLE=table

            The table to delete from.  The default is the process table.
            Deleting a table's name from LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY or
            LNM$SYSTEM_DIRECTORY deletes the table and all its descendants:

                 DEASSIGN/TABLE=LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY TAX

        /USER_MODE, /SUPERVISOR_MODE, /EXECUTIVE_MODE

            Delete names at this access mode and any less privileged
            mode.  The default is supervisor mode, so the executive-mode
            names SYS$INPUT, SYS$OUTPUT, SYS$ERROR and SYS$COMMAND are not
            deleted.

        /ALL

            Delete every such name in the table.

$CREA,/NAM
    The CREATE/NAME_TABLE command creates a logical name table.

    Syntax:

         CREATE/NAME_TABLE table-name

    Qualifiers:

        /PARENT_TABLE=table

            The new table's parent.  The default, LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY,
            makes a table private to the process.  A shareable parent
            such as LNM$SYSTEM_DIRECTORY makes a shareable table.

        /USER_MODE, /SUPERVISOR_MODE, /EXECUTIVE_MODE

            The access mode of the table.  The default is supervisor mode.

        /LOG (default), /NOLOG

            Report when an existing table is superseded.

    To have file lookups search the new table, add it to LNM$PROCESS:

         $ DEFINE/TABLE=LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY LNM$PROCESS -
                LNM$PROCESS_TABLE, TAX

$CREA,/DIR
    The CREATE/DIRECTORY command creates a directory on a mounted ODS-2
    volume, as VMS DCL's CREATE/DIRECTORY does, and every directory above
    it that doesn't exist yet: CREATE/DIRECTORY [A.B.C] makes [A], [A.B],
    and [A.B.C], whichever are missing.  A directory that already exists
    is reported, and isn't an error.

    Syntax:

        CREATE/DIRECTORY [qualifiers] <directory>[,<directory>...]

    Parameter:

        <directory>     A directory specification, such as [A.B] or
                        DUA0:[A.B].  It may be relative to the current
                        default directory ([.SUB], [-.OTHER]; see SET
                        DEFAULT), and its device may be a logical name.
                        It can't name a file, or use wildcards.  With no
                        device and no default device on a mounted volume,
                        the directory is made on the host instead,
                        relative to the host's current directory.

    Qualifiers:

        /OWNER_UIC=<uic>
        /OWNER_UIC=PARENT
                        The new directory's owner: a UIC, [group,member]
                        in octal, or PARENT for the owner of the
                        directory it's made in, which is also the
                        default.

        /VERSION_LIMIT=<n>
                        The default version limit for files created in
                        the new directory; 0 means no limit.  The default
                        is the limit of the directory it's made in.

        /PROTECTION=(<category>:<access>,...)
                        The new directory's protection: for each of
                        SYSTEM, OWNER, GROUP, and WORLD (or S, O, G, W),
                        the access granted, any of R, W, E, and D, or
                        none.  The default is the protection of the
                        directory it's made in, less delete access; a
                        category not given keeps that default.

        /ALLOCATION=<n> The number of blocks to give the directory at
                        once.  The default is 1.

        /LOG            Reports the directory made.  The directories
                        made above it aren't reported.

    A /VERSION_LIMIT out of range is reported and ignored, and the
    directory still made, as VMS does.

    Examples:

        CREATE/DIRECTORY [PROJECTS.TEST]

        CREATE/DIRECTORY/LOG/OWNER_UIC=[200,201]/VERSION_LIMIT=3 [USER]

        CREATE/DIRECTORY/PROTECTION=(S:RWE,O:RWE,G:RE,W) [.SHARED]

    See also the help for DIRECTORY and SET DEFAULT.

$CREA
    The CREATE command creates a file, as VMS DCL's CREATE does, from
    the lines of SYS$INPUT: at the terminal, the lines typed up to
    Ctrl/Z; in a command procedure, the data lines after the CREATE
    command, up to the next line that starts with "$" (or the end of a
    DECK).  The file is created even when there are no lines.

    Syntax:

        CREATE [/LOG] <file>[/HOST]

    Parameter:

        <file>          The file to create.  A file on a mounted ODS-2
                        volume, such as DUA0:[WORK]NOTES.TXT or, with
                        SET DEFAULT on a volume, NOTES.TXT, is a
                        variable-length record file with carriage-return
                        carriage control, as VMS makes a text file; it
                        is a new version if the file already exists.  A
                        host file makes a plain host text file instead,
                        each line ending as the host's do: LF on Unix
                        and macOS, CR LF on Windows.  A name with a host
                        path ("./notes.txt", quoted to keep its case) is
                        a host file; so is a bare name when the default
                        directory isn't on a mounted volume.

    Qualifiers:

        /HOST           Attached to <file>, makes it a host file name,
                        whatever it looks like: CREATE "notes.txt"/HOST.

        /LOG            Report the file created, with its version.

    Examples:

        CREATE NOTES.TXT
        This is the first line.
        This is the last.
        ^Z

        $ CREATE/LOG DUA0:[WORK]SETUP.DAT
        ITEM ONE
        ITEM TWO
        $ TYPE DUA0:[WORK]SETUP.DAT

    CREATE has two other forms, each its own command:

        CREATE/DIRECTORY    creates a directory on a mounted volume.
        CREATE/NAME_TABLE   creates a logical name table.

    For details, see:

        HELP CREATE /DIRECTORY
        HELP CREATE /NAME_TABLE

    See also the help for TYPE and SET DEFAULT.

$SHOW,DEVI
$SH  ,DEVI
$SHOW,DEV 
$SH  ,DEV 
    The SHOW DEVICE command displays information about a pseudodevice
    attached to the VMS system.  These devices are emulated via VMS
    system service calls that are managed by the emulator when a full
    version of VMS is not running.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW DEVICE [/FULL] [name]
    
    Parameter:
    
        name
        
            The name of a device to display, or the start of one: SHOW
            DEVICE DU shows every device whose name begins with DU
            (DUA0, DUA1, ...).  If no device's does, the command says
            %SYSTEM-W-NOSUCHDEV.  If omitted then all known devices are
            displayed.
        
        /FULL
        
            If this qualifier is present then all information about
            the device (essentially the device control block contents)
            is displayed.  For a disk with a volume mounted, "Operations
            completed" is the number of block reads and writes done on
            the volume since it was mounted, and the size, free space,
            label, and file count are read from the volume itself.
 
$CLEA,MEMO
$CLEA,MEM
$ZERO
    The CLEAR MEMORY command (or ZERO command, which is a synonym) is used
    to zero out the virtual VAX's memory.  All physical memory is cleared,
    which implicitly zeros out virtual memory.  When a CLEAR MEMORY command
    is given, it also turns virtual memory off, since the page tables will 
    have been zeroed.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR MEMORY

$CLEA
    The CLEAR command is used to clear various attributes of the virtual VAX
    or the console processor.  See the help for

        CLEAR MEMORY
        CLEAR STRINGS

    Breakpoints, interrupts, the translation buffer, and the machine's symbol
    table (CLEAR SYMBOL) are the debugger's: see CANCEL in the debugger's
    help (DEBUG, then HELP CANCEL).  A DCL symbol is deleted with
    DELETE/SYMBOL.

$INIT

    The INITIALIZE command performs one of two unrelated operations,
    selected by a required qualifier -- INITIALIZE (or its unambiguous
    4-letter abbreviation, INIT) with no qualifier at all is an error.

        INITIALIZE/VAX          (Re-)configures the amount of physical
                                 RAM the virtual VAX is running with.

        INITIALIZE/CONTAINER    Formats a new, empty ODS-2 disk-image
                                 container file for use with MOUNT.

    See the help for INITIALIZE/VAX and INITIALIZE/CONTAINER for the
    syntax of each.

$INIT,/VAX

    The INITIALIZE/VAX command (re-)initializes the virtual VAX, and
    specifies the amount of physical RAM the machine is configured with.
    This storage is allocated by the govax process from its own address
    space.

    Syntax:

        INITIALIZE/VAX <pages>

    Parameter:

        <pages>         The number of physical pages of VAX memory that the
                        virtual machine is configured with.  Each page is
                        512 bytes, so a page count of 1024 would require a
                        half-megabyte virtual machine, for example.

    Example:

        INITIALIZE/VAX 8192   ; Create a 4mb virtual VAX

    See also the help for INITIALIZE and INITIALIZE/CONTAINER.

$INIT,/CON

    The INITIALIZE/CONTAINER command formats a new, empty ODS-2 disk-image
    container file -- a real Files-11 volume, compatible with real VAX/VMS
    and other emulators such as simh.  The new container is not mounted;
    use MOUNT afterward to attach it to a device before using it.

    Syntax:

        INITIALIZE/CONTAINER <file> [label] [/SIZE=<n>] [/DEVICE=<dev>] [/CLUSTER=<n>]

    Parameters:

        <file>          The path to the new disk-image container file on
                        the native file system.  If the native file system
                        is case sensitive, or the name contains embedded
                        blanks, it should be enclosed in quotes to preserve
                        case and spacing.

        <size>          The size of the new volume, in 512-byte blocks.

        [label]         The volume label to record in the container's home
                        block.  If omitted, the volume is labeled NONAME.

    Qualifier:

        /CLUSTER=<n>    The number of blocks per storage cluster.  If
                        omitted, the volume uses 1-block clusters.

        /DEVICE=<dev>   Specify the VAX device type for this container,
                        which sets the maximum block count.
        
        /SIZE=<n>       Specify the maximum block count for the container.
                        This is required if /DEVICE is not specified.

    Example:

        INITIALIZE/CONTAINER "newdisk.dsk" /DEVICE=RD54 WORK01

    See also the help for INITIALIZE, INITIALIZE/VAX, and MOUNT.

$EXIT

    The EXIT command ends the command procedure it is in, and the
    command level that ran the procedure goes on.  At the terminal
    (command level 0), EXIT ends govax, as QUIT does.

    Syntax:

        EXIT [status]

    Parameters:

        status      An expression (see HELP SYMBOL EXPRESSIONS) whose
                    integer value is the procedure's status, the $STATUS
                    of the level that ran it.  A status that isn't a
                    success (an even number) has its message shown there,
                    unless its bit 28 says it has been shown already.
                    Without a status, the procedure's status is $STATUS,
                    marked as shown.  See HELP SYMBOL STATUS.

    Example:

        $ EXIT 1            ! end the procedure, successfully

$ON

    The ON command says what a command procedure does when one of its
    commands fails, or when CTRL/Y is pressed while it runs.  Without
    one, a procedure ends when a command fails with an error or a fatal
    error, and goes on after a warning.

    Syntax:

        ON condition THEN [$] command

    Parameters:

        condition   WARNING         a warning, an error, or a fatal error
                    ERROR           an error or a fatal error
                    SEVERE_ERROR    a fatal error
                    CONTROL_Y       CTRL/Y pressed while the
                                    procedure runs

        command     The command to run in place of the procedure's usual
                    action, such as EXIT, CONTINUE, or SHOW SYMBOL
                    $STATUS.

    The command runs once: after it, the usual action is back until the
    next ON.  ON CONTROL_Y is the exception: its command runs for each
    CTRL/Y until another ON CONTROL_Y, SET NOCONTROL=Y, or the end of
    the procedure.  If an image is running when CTRL/Y is pressed, it
    is stopped first; a command of DCL's own completes first, and the
    procedure goes on after it unless the command (GOTO, EXIT, STOP)
    goes elsewhere.  ON applies only to the command level it is in, so a
    procedure the procedure runs has its own.  SET NOON turns the
    checking off, and SET ON (or an ON) back on.  At the terminal, ON
    is accepted and does nothing.

    Example:

        $ ON WARNING THEN EXIT
        $ ON ERROR THEN CONTINUE
        $ ON CONTROL_Y THEN GOTO CLEAN_UP

$CONT

    The CONTINUE command resumes a command procedure that CTRL/Y (or
    CTRL/C) interrupted, and the image it was running, if one was (see
    HELP KEYS).  Anywhere else it does nothing, and leaves $STATUS as it
    was: in a command procedure, ON ERROR THEN CONTINUE makes the
    procedure go on after an error.

    Syntax:

        CONTINUE

$QUIT

    The QUIT command ends govax, from the terminal or from inside a
    command procedure.

    Syntax:

        QUIT

$LOAD

    The LOAD command reads the contents of a saved memory image file
    into the current virtual VAX.  The LOAD command restores the contents
    of memory and the registers.  It does not restore saved symbols,
    breakpoints, or other console-specific context.   Also see the help
    for the LOAD/ROM command is used to load a ROM image into the console 
    ROM memory, and LOAD/NVRAM to load non-volatile RAM images.

    Syntax:

        LOAD    <file-name>

        
        
    Parameter:

        <file-name>     The name of the file to be read.  If
                        omitted, the default name of "Saved.VAX"
                        is assumed.
        
        
$LOAD,/ROM

    The LOAD/ROM command reads a ROM file into the special memory area
    in the virtual VAX physical address space for a console ROM.  This
    ROM file can be a text file containing the console EXAM command
    output of the console memory, or a binary image created with the
    SAVE/ROM command.

    Syntax:


        LOAD/ROM [/BASE=<base-addr>] [/SIZE=<size-in-k>] <filename>
        
            or
        
        LOAD/ROM/NOERROR
        
        
    Parameters:

        <file-name>     The name of the file to be read.  If
                        omitted, the default name of "default.rom"
                        is assumed.
        
        <base-addr>     When loading a ROM image, this specifies
                        the base physical address of the ROM image
                        to use.  If ommitted, 20040000 is assumed.
        
        <size-in-k>     This is the size of the ROM memory in kilobytes.
                        If ommitted, the default is ^d256 for a 256K ROM.

        Note that /BASE and /SIZE cannot be used if the ROM file being
        read is a ROM image created by SAVE/ROM; this has a fixed address
        and size associated with it.
        
        The second example (using the /NOERROR qualifier) indicates that if
        the default ROM file cannot be found then no error is displayed.  This
        allows the LOAD/ROM/NOERROR command to be placed in the initialization
        file, and not interfere with startup if a ROM file is not found.
$LOAD,/NVR

    The LOAD/NVRAM command reads a non-volatile RAM file into the special 
    memory area in the virtual VAX physical address space for the NVRAM.
    The NVRAM area exists on some VAX systems to contain console boot data,
    etc. and is used by the console ROM on those systems.  The NVRAM file
    is created with the SAVE/NVRAM command after INIT/NVRAM has first
    allocated it.
    

    Syntax:


        LOAD/NVRAM  <filename>
        
            or
        
        LOAD/NVRAM/NOERROR
        
        
    Parameters:

        <file-name>     The name of the file to be read.  If
                        omitted, the default name of "default.nvram"
                        is assumed.
        
        
        The second example (using the /NOERROR qualifier) indicates that if
        the default NVRAM file cannot be found then no error is displayed.  This
        allows the LOAD/NVRAM/NOERROR command to be placed in the initialization
        file, and not interfere with startup if a NVRAM file is not found.
$INIT,/NVR

    The INIT/NVRAM command creates an empty non-volatile RAM area for use with
    a console ROM file.  The console ROM should contain code to initialize the
    NVRAM to a consistent state.  Use the SAVE/NVRAM command to save the resulting
    NVRAM file.  
    

    Syntax:


        INIT/NVRAM [/BASE=<base-addr>] [/SIZE=<size-in-k>]
                
        
    Parameters:

        
        <base-addr>     When creating an NVRAM area, this specifies
                        the base physical address of the NVRAM
                        to use.  If ommitted, 20080000 is assumed.
        
        <size-in-k>     This is the size of the NVRAM memory in kilobytes.
                        If ommitted, the default is 1 for a 1K NVRAM.

$SAVE

    The SAVE command writes the current memory contents to a binary file.
    This file can later be reloaded with the LOAD command.  For help on
    saving the entire machine state to a text file, see the help on
    SAVE/TEXT.  You can also use the SAVE/ROM command to save a ROM image.

    Note that the memory image for the SAVE command is not just a file
    image of the memory.  It contains a header of approxamately 1K of
    data describing the state of the virtual VAX at the time the SAVE
    command is issued.  This is followed by zero or more page ranges,
    where the address and number of pages is stored followed by that
    many pages of data.  The SAVE command only saves non-zero pages.
    
    Syntax:

        SAVE  <file-path>

    Parameter:

        <file-name>     This is the name of the file that the memory
                        image is saved to.  If omitted, the default
                        name of "Saved.VAX" is assumed.

    See also:

        SAVE/ROM        Save the current ROM storage as a file
        SAVE/NVRAM      Save the current NVRAM storage as a file

$SAVE,/ROM

    The SAVE/ROM command writes system ROM to an external file.

    Syntax:

        SAVE/ROM <file-path>

    Parameter:

        <file-path>     This is the name of the file where the emulator
                        ROM image is to be saved.  The name can be enclosed in
                        quotation marks if it contains imbedded blanks.
                        The file will be overwritten if it already exists.
    
    Example:

        SAVE/ROM "current.rom"

$SAVE,/NVR

    The SAVE/NVRAM command writes system NVRAM storage to an external file.

    Syntax:

        SAVE/NVRAM <file-path>

    Parameter:

        <file-path>     This is the name of the file where the emulator
                        NVRAM image is to be saved.  The name can be enclosed in
                        quotation marks if it contains imbedded blanks.
                        The file will be overwritten if it already exists.
    
    Example:

        SAVE/NVRAM "latest.nvram"


$@   

    The @ command (the execute procedure command) runs a command
    procedure: a file of DCL commands.  The procedure runs at a new
    command level, one deeper than the level that ran it; the terminal is
    command level 0, and procedures may nest 32 levels deep.

    Syntax:

        @file-spec[/OUTPUT=file-spec] [p1 [p2 ... p8]]

    Parameters:

        file-spec       The command procedure: a file on a mounted volume
                        or on the host, by the same rules as every
                        console command's file names.  The file type
                        defaults to .COM.  A host file name with
                        lowercase letters or a "/" must be quoted.

        p1 ... p8       Up to eight parameters, separated by blanks.  The
                        procedure sees them as the local symbols P1 to
                        P8; the ones not given are "".  Letters are
                        uppercased unless quoted; quotes keep blanks and
                        case, and "" passes an empty parameter.

    Qualifier:

        /OUTPUT=file    Sends the procedure's output to the file instead
                        of the terminal (.LIS by default; NL: discards
                        it).  Error messages go to the terminal too.  The
                        qualifier must follow the file name with no blank
                        between them; otherwise it is a parameter.

    In the procedure, each command line starts with "$", and "$!" starts
    a comment line.  A line ending in "-" continues on the next line,
    which has no "$".  A line without a "$" is data, for whatever reads
    the procedure's input (SYS$INPUT): an image the procedure runs (with
    LIB$GET_INPUT, RMS's $GET, or a terminal $QIO read), TYPE SYS$INPUT,
    the debugger, once a DEBUG or RUN/DEBUG in the procedure has started
    it, or the interactive assembler.  An image reading the data comes to
    its end of file at the next "$" line; DECK lets data lines start with
    "$" (see HELP DECK).  Data that nothing reads is skipped after an
    image, with a SKPDAT warning, up to the next "$" line (a lone "$"
    will do); after one of DCL's own commands (an assignment, IF, SET
    VERIFY, ...), a data line is read as a command.

    Each command level has its own local symbols (assigned with "=" or
    ":="), which go when the procedure ends; global symbols ("==" and
    ":==") stay.  A procedure sees the local symbols of the levels that
    ran it.

    The procedure ends at its last line, at an EXIT, or when a command
    fails with an error or a fatal error (a warning doesn't end it).  An
    error ends the procedures that ran it too.  ON and SET [NO]ON change
    what a failure does, and EXIT can give the procedure's status (see
    HELP ON, HELP EXIT, and HELP SYMBOL STATUS).

    Example:

        @"setup.com" "Test Run" fast

        where setup.com is:

        $! Show the parameters this procedure was given.
        $ SHOW SYMBOL P1
        $ SHOW SYMBOL P2

        shows P1 = "Test Run" and P2 = "FAST".

$RUN 
$RUN ,/DEB
    The RUN command loads a VAX/VMS image into memory, and executes it.  This
    command assumes that a VMINIT has been run, and the standard micro-kernel
    (kernel.asm) has been loaded and initialized.  
    
    The command will load a valid, normal VAX/VMS image into memory.  References
    to sharable images must be resolved using "software shims".  These are stubs
    that map sharable image vector slots to routines in memory, typically supplied
    by the micro-kernel.  Some shims may redirect control to native runtime code
    via a special XFC instruction.  See the help on ASM PSEUDO SHIM or SHOW SHIM
    for more information.
    
    If the image has a transfer address, then the image is started at it's user
    transfer address.  If there is no transfer address, the image is loaded but
    not executed.  
    
    If the optional /DEBUG qualifier is used, the image is loaded and then run
    under the debugger, which stops it at the first instruction of its main
    routine (after the entry mask) and prompts with DBG>.  GO continues it;
    STEP, SET BREAK, EXAMINE, and the rest are the debugger's commands.  An
    image linked with /DEBUG (LINK/DEBUG) starts the debugger the same way,
    unless RUN/NODEBUG says not to.  An image linked /NOTRACEBACK has no symbols for
    the debugger, and runs without it.  /STEP and /BREAK are
    synonyms of /DEBUG, and /NOEXECUTE loads the image without running it.
    
    Syntax:
    
        RUN [/DEBUG|/NODEBUG] [/NOEXECUTE] <image-name>[/HOST]
    
    Parameter:
    
        <image-name>    An image file, on the native system or on a mounted
                        ODS-2 volume, by the same rules as MACRO's and LINK's
                        files (see HELP MACRO): a VMS file specification
                        (DUA0:[PROGS]HELLO.EXE), or a bare name while SET
                        DEFAULT is on a volume, is a file on the volume, with
                        the type EXE if it has none.  Anything else, or any
                        name followed by /HOST, is a native file.  An image
                        made on a VAX can be copied to the native system in
                        binary mode, or read in place from its disk.
                        
                        If the native file system is case sensitive, the name may 
                        need to be enclosed in quotes to preserve case.
                        
                        The image is mapped into memory and executed.  The
                        shareable images it uses are always looked for on
                        the native system; govax's shims stand in for any
                        that aren't there.

    RUN gives the image no command text: LIB$GET_FOREIGN returns an empty
    string.  To pass an image text, run it as a foreign command (see HELP
    FOREIGN), or give the text after the file name on govax's own command
    line ("govax run forth.exe 2 3 + .").

$SPAWN
    SPAWN creates a subprocess of the console's process, as DCL's SPAWN and
    a program's LIB$SPAWN do, and (unless /NOWAIT) waits for it to end.  It
    needs vax.process.scheduler on, as it is unless the configuration turns
    it off (see HELP CONFIG KEYS).

    Syntax:

        SPAWN [/NOWAIT] [/INPUT=file] [/OUTPUT=file] [/PROCESS=name]
              [/PROMPT=string] [/NOSYMBOLS] [/NOLOGICAL_NAMES] [command]

    The subprocess runs DCL, the same command interpreter as the console:
    it runs the command and logs out, or, with no command, reads commands
    from its input until the end of the input, LOGOUT, or EXIT.  Every DCL
    command works there, command procedures (@) included, with the
    subprocess's own symbols (copies of the console's, unless /NOSYMBOLS),
    logical names (copies of its process logical names, unless
    /NOLOGICAL_NAMES), default directory, $STATUS, and open files.  The
    commands that work the machine itself (DEBUG, ASM, INITIALIZE/VAX,
    VMINIT, ZERO, SAVE, LOAD, ...) are the console's own, and give
    %VAX-E-NOTSUBPROC.  A subprocess can SPAWN a subprocess of its own,
    which it waits for (unless /NOWAIT) as the console does; a process's
    subprocesses end when it does.  SPAWN says when the subprocess has
    been made (%DCL-S-SPAWNED, and %DCL-S-ATTACHED if it waits) and when
    control is back (%DCL-S-RETURNED), as DCL does.  SPAWN's status is
    the subprocess's final status.

        /NOWAIT         Don't wait: the subprocess runs beside the console.
                        It takes turns with the console's DCL, between its
                        commands (one command each, in a procedure), during
                        WAIT, while the machine runs (RUN, GO, CALL), and
                        while the VAX> prompt waits for a line.  What it
                        writes meanwhile appears above the line being
                        typed, which is shown again below it.  (With input
                        from a pipe, not a terminal, it runs at the prompt
                        only until it waits or has run a while.)
        /INPUT=file     The subprocess's SYS$INPUT: the terminal, a mailbox,
                        or a file of commands.  Default: the console's.
        /OUTPUT=file    Its SYS$OUTPUT: a terminal, a mailbox, NL:, or a
                        file, which gets each line as a record.
        /PROCESS=name   Its name.  Default: the user name and "_n".
        /PROMPT=string  The prompt for commands read from the terminal.
                        Default: "$ ".

    Example:

        VAX> HELLO :== $DUA0:[WORK]HELLO.EXE
        VAX> SPAWN HELLO
        VAX> SPAWN DIRECTORY
        VAX> SPAWN/NOWAIT @NIGHTLY

    A SPAWN that waits gives the subprocess the terminal.  ATTACH gives it
    back, or to another process of the console's job (see HELP ATTACH).

$ATTACH

    ATTACH gives the terminal to another process of the console's job and
    waits until it comes back, as DCL's ATTACH and a program's LIB$ATTACH
    do.  The process must be waiting for the terminal itself: a process
    that SPAWNed a subprocess and waits for it, or one that gave the
    terminal away by ATTACH.  A SPAWN/NOWAIT subprocess, the process
    typing ATTACH, and a process of another job are refused
    (%LIB-F-ATTREQREF); a process that isn't there is %SYSTEM-W-NONEXPR.

    Syntax:

        ATTACH process-name
        ATTACH/IDENTIFICATION=pid

    The terminal comes back when another process ATTACHes to the waiting
    one, or when the process that has it logs out (it then goes to that
    process's parent).  DCL then says so (%DCL-S-RETURNED).  A SPAWN that
    waits is over, its subprocess still there, when the subprocess
    ATTACHes back to its parent.

    Example:

        VAX> SPAWN
        $ ATTACH SYSTEM
        %DCL-S-RETURNED, control returned to process SYSTEM
        VAX> ATTACH SYSTEM_1
        %DCL-S-RETURNED, control returned to process SYSTEM_1
        $ LOGOUT

$LOGOUT

    In a subprocess (see HELP SPAWN), LOGOUT ends its DCL and the process,
    which says so ("Process name logged out at ...").  The process's final
    status is $STATUS's.  In the console's own process it is QUIT.

    Syntax:

        LOGOUT

$MCR

    MCR runs an image, as a foreign command does, with the rest of the
    line as the text LIB$GET_FOREIGN returns.

    Syntax:

        MCR image [text]

    Example:

        VAX> MCR DUA0:[WORK]ECHO hello there

$SYMB
    A symbol assignment gives a name to a string, as DCL's do.  When the first
    word of a command is a symbol, its value replaces the word before the
    command is read.  A value beginning with "$" makes the symbol a foreign
    command (see HELP FOREIGN); any other value is the start of a command, so
    a symbol can abbreviate or rename one.

    Syntax:

        symbol-name :=  string            symbol-name =  expression
        symbol-name :== string            symbol-name == expression

    The string is the rest of the line: outside quotes, letters are made
    uppercase, runs of blanks become one, and a "!" begins a comment; quotes
    keep text as written, and are removed ("" inside them is a quote).  The
    expression is DCL's: integers, quoted strings, symbols, and lexical
    functions, with arithmetic, string, comparison, and logical operators
    (see HELP SYMBOL EXPRESSIONS).  Its value, an integer or a string, is
    the symbol's.  To use a symbol's value elsewhere in a command, put it
    between apostrophes (see HELP SYMBOL SUBSTITUTION).

    An "*" in the name says how short an abbreviation still means the symbol:
    after "FO*RTH :== $FORTH", FO, FOR, FORT, and FORTH all do.

    ":=" and "=" make a local symbol, ":==" and "==" a global one.  Local
    symbols belong to the current command level: the terminal's, or the
    command procedure's (see HELP @), and go when it ends.  A local symbol
    is found before a global one of the same name.  SHOW SYMBOL displays
    them, and DELETE/SYMBOL deletes them.  These are DCL symbols, not the
    machine's symbol table that ASM and the debugger use (see the
    debugger's HELP SHOW SYMBOL).

    Examples:

        SD :== SET DEFAULT
        FORTH :== $DUA0:[TOOLS]FORTH.EXE
        GREETING == "Hello, ""World"""
        TOTAL = COUNT + 1
        LEN = F$LENGTH(GREETING)

$SYMB,EXPR
$SYM ,EXPR
    The value of an "=" or "==" assignment is an expression, as in DCL.  Its
    operands are:

        integers       13, -5, %X1F (hexadecimal), %O17 (octal), %D10
        strings        "in quotes", with "" for a quote inside
        symbols        a name, replaced by the symbol's value
        functions      F$LENGTH(...) and the other lexical functions
                       (see HELP LEXICAL)

    The operators, from the first done to the last; parentheses group:

        + -            unary plus and minus
        * /            multiplication and division (integers; no fraction)
        + -            addition and subtraction; on two strings,
                       concatenation and removing the first occurrence
        .EQ. .NE. .LT. .LE. .GT. .GE.         compare numbers
        .EQS. .NES. .LTS. .LES. .GTS. .GES.   compare strings
        .NOT.          inverts every bit
        .AND.          bitwise and
        .OR.           bitwise or

    A comparison is 1 when true and 0 when false.  Where an integer is
    needed, a string that holds a number is that number, one beginning with
    T or Y (either case) is 1, and any other is 0.  Where a string is needed,
    an integer is its decimal digits.  Integers are 32 bits and wrap around.
    In a test, odd is true and even false.

    Examples:

        $ BALANCE = 142 * 14
        $ WEEKEND = "Saturday, " + "Sunday"
        $ BIG = COUNT .GT. 10 .AND. NAME .EQS. "TOTAL"

$SYMB,SUBS
$SYM ,SUBS
    A symbol's value takes the place of its name in a command when the name
    is between apostrophes:

        $ TYPE 'FILE'
        $ @NEXT 'P1' 'P2'

    Inside quotes, the name takes two apostrophes before it and one after:
    "Creating ''NAME'.DAT".  A lexical function can be between apostrophes
    too: 'F$LENGTH(X)'.  Outside quotes, the closing apostrophe may be left
    out at the end of a word ('NAME), and a value that itself has a symbol
    between apostrophes is replaced in turn.  An undefined symbol is
    replaced by nothing.  This happens before anything else reads the
    command, so the value can be any part of it, the verb included.  The
    value is then made uppercase with the rest of the command, unless it's
    inside quotes.

    &NAME is also replaced by the symbol's value, later: after the command's
    first word is looked up as a symbol, and after the command is made
    uppercase, so the value keeps its case, and it stays one parameter even
    with blanks in it.  It must follow a blank or a special character, and
    does nothing inside quotes, in a ":=" assignment, or in an @ command's
    parameters.

        $ COUNT = 1
        $ TYPE &P'COUNT'          ! 'COUNT' first, giving &P1

    Data lines in a procedure, and debugger commands, are not substituted.

$SYMB,STAT
$SYM ,STAT
    Each command ends with a status, a VMS condition value, which DCL keeps
    in the global symbol $STATUS, and its severity in $SEVERITY:

        $ SHOW SYMBOL $STATUS
          $STATUS == "%X00030001"

    The low three bits are the severity: 0 a warning, 1 success, 2 an
    error, 3 informational, 4 fatal.  So $STATUS is odd (true) after a
    command that succeeded:

        $ IF $STATUS THEN PRINT "it worked"

    Bit 28 set means the status's message has been shown already, so it
    isn't shown again.  A command that succeeds gives %X00030001; one that
    fails gives VMS's condition value for its message, where VMS has one.
    RUN gives the image's exit status (showing its message if it failed),
    and @ the procedure's (see HELP EXIT).  SHOW SYMBOL, IF, and CONTINUE
    leave $STATUS as it was when they succeed.  In a command procedure, the
    status decides whether the procedure goes on (see HELP ON).

$LEXI
    A lexical function returns an integer or a string, in an expression
    (see HELP SYMBOL EXPRESSIONS) or between apostrophes.  Its arguments are
    expressions, in parentheses and separated by commas; an optional one may
    be left out, keeping its comma, and a function with no arguments still
    takes "()".  Its name may be shortened while it names only one of VMS's
    lexical functions (F$LEN is F$LENGTH; F$L is ambiguous).  A keyword
    argument (an item, a field) is spelled in full, in either case.

    Strings and data types:

        F$CVSI(bit, size, string)        a bit field, signed
        F$CVUI(bit, size, string)        a bit field, unsigned
        F$EDIT(string, edits)            COLLAPSE, COMPRESS, LOWERCASE,
                                         TRIM, UNCOMMENT, UPCASE (a list)
        F$ELEMENT(n, delimiter, string)  the nth element, from 0
        F$EXTRACT(offset, length, string)  part of a string, from 0
        F$FAO(control, arg...)           $FAO's directives (!AS, !UL, ...)
        F$INTEGER(expression)            the value as an integer
        F$LENGTH(string)                 the string's length
        F$LOCATE(substring, string)      its offset, or the length if absent
        F$STRING(expression)             the value as a string
        F$TYPE(symbol)                   INTEGER, STRING, PROCESS_CONTEXT,
                                         or "" (undefined)

    The process and its command environment:

        F$DIRECTORY()                    the default directory
        F$ENVIRONMENT(item)              DEPTH, PROCEDURE, DEFAULT, PROMPT,
                                         ON_SEVERITY, ON_CONTROL_Y,
                                         VERIFY_PROCEDURE, VERIFY_IMAGE,
                                         INTERACTIVE, MAX_DEPTH, PROTECTION,
                                         ...
        F$GETJPI(pid, item)              $GETJPI's items ("" for this
                                         process): PID, PRCNAM, USERNAME,
                                         UIC, CURPRIV, STATE, MODE, ...
        F$GETSYI(item [,node])           $GETSYI's items: NODENAME, VERSION,
                                         BOOTTIME, SID, ...
        F$IDENTIFIER(identifier, type)   NAME_TO_NUMBER: an identifier's
                                         value (0 if there's none);
                                         NUMBER_TO_NAME: its name ("")
        F$MODE()                         INTERACTIVE
        F$PID(context)                   each process's PID in turn, then
                                         "" (set context to "" first)
        F$CONTEXT("PROCESS", context, item [,value] [,qualifier])
                                         makes F$PID return only the
                                         processes whose item (PRCNAM,
                                         USERNAME, OWNER, GRP, PRI, UIC,
                                         CURPRIV, STATE, MODE, ...) matches
                                         a value: EQL or NEQ, for a number
                                         LSS, LEQ, GTR, GEQ, for CURPRIV
                                         ALL or ANY; item CANCEL ends it
        F$PRIVILEGE(privileges)          TRUE if they're enabled (and those
                                         named NOxxx aren't)
        F$PROCESS()                      the process's name
        F$SETPRV(privileges)             enables (NOxxx disables); returns
                                         what they were
        F$USER()                         the UIC, "[SYSTEM]"
        F$VERIFY([procedure] [,image])   returns verification (1 or 0),
                                         and sets it

    Files, devices, logical names, messages, and time:

        F$GETDVI(device, item)           $GETDVI's items: EXISTS, MNT,
                                         VOLNAM, FREEBLOCKS, DEVNAM, ...
        F$DEVICE([name] [,class] [,type] [,stream])
                                         the next device whose name matches
                                         (with * and %), of the class (DISK,
                                         TERM, MAILBOX, ...) and type, then
                                         ""
        F$FILE_ATTRIBUTES(file, item)    an attribute from the file's
                                         header: ORG, RFM, RAT, MRS, LRL,
                                         ALQ, EOF, FFB, CDT, RDT, PRO, UIC,
                                         FID, DID, DIRECTORY, VERLIMIT, ...
        F$PARSE(file [,default] [,related] [,field] [,type])
                                         the full file specification, or a
                                         field: NODE, DEVICE, DIRECTORY,
                                         NAME, TYPE, VERSION; type
                                         SYNTAX_ONLY or NO_CONCEAL
        F$SEARCH(file [,stream])         the next file that matches, then ""
        F$LOGICAL(name)                  a logical name's translation
        F$TRNLNM(name [,table] [,index] [,mode] [,case] [,item])
                                         a translation, or an item:
                                         VALUE, MAX_INDEX, TABLE_NAME,
                                         ACCESS_MODE, CONCEALED, LENGTH, ...
        F$MESSAGE(status)                a status's message text
        F$TIME()                         the date and time now
        F$CVTIME([time] [,format] [,field])
                                         a time in COMPARISON (yyyy-mm-dd
                                         hh:mm:ss.cc), ABSOLUTE, or DELTA
                                         format, or a field: DATE, TIME,
                                         YEAR, MONTH, DAY, WEEKDAY, ...

    F$CSID, F$GETQUI, and F$LICENSE are not here: govax has no cluster,
    queues, or licenses for them to report.

    govax has one lexical function VMS doesn't, for its configuration (see
    HELP CONFIG KEYS):

        F$CONFIG(key [,operation])       VALUE (the default): the item's
                                         value, or "" if it isn't set;
                                         EXISTS: 1 if it's set, else 0

    The key is a configuration item's name, such as "vax.startup.procedure",
    in either case.

    Examples:

        $ LEN = F$LENGTH(NAME) + 1
        $ GROUP = F$EXTRACT(1, F$LOCATE(",", UIC) - 1, UIC)
        $ IF F$SEARCH("TEMP.DAT") .NES. "" THEN DELETE TEMP.DAT;*
        $ IF F$TRNLNM("NAMES") .EQS. "" THEN DEFINE NAMES EMPLOYEES.DAT
        $ WRITE_TIME = F$CVTIME(,,"TIME")
        $ IF F$CONFIG("vax.library", "EXISTS") THEN -
              WRITE SYS$OUTPUT F$CONFIG("vax.library")

$FORE
    A foreign command runs an image with the rest of the command line as its
    command text, which the image reads with LIB$GET_FOREIGN.  Define one with
    a symbol whose value is "$" and the image's file name (see HELP SYMBOL):

        FORTH :== $DUA0:[TOOLS]FORTH.EXE
        FORTH 2 3 + . CR

    The image is found as RUN finds it.  The command text is treated as DCL
    treats a command's parameters: outside quotes it is made uppercase and its
    blanks are compressed, and a "!" begins a comment; quoted text, quotes and
    all, is passed as typed.

$DELE,/SYM
$DEL ,/SYM
    The DELETE/SYMBOL command deletes a symbol (see HELP SYMBOL).

    Syntax:

        DELETE/SYMBOL [/LOCAL | /GLOBAL] symbol-name
        DELETE/SYMBOL [/LOCAL | /GLOBAL] /ALL

    The name may be any abbreviation the symbol allows.

    Qualifiers:

        /LOCAL          Delete from the local symbol table of the current
                        command level (the default).
        /GLOBAL         Delete from the global symbol table.
        /ALL            Delete every symbol in the table, instead of one.

$SET 

    The SET command is used to set the state of information in the virtual
    VAX or the console processor.  See the help text on:

    CONTROL     DEBUG       DEFAULT     ON
    PREFIX      PROMPT      QUANTUM     RADIX
    VERIFY      WORKING_SET

    A symbol is set with DCL's assignments (see HELP SYMBOL).

    The settings of the machine itself (breakpoints, stepping, the PSL, page
    table entries, the base address, the display modes) are the debugger's:
    see HELP SET in the debugger (type DEBUG, then HELP SET).

$SET ,QUAN
    The SET QUANTUM command sets the internal interrupt interval value, 
    expressed as the number of instrutions that must pass before an
    interrupt or process context switch can occur. Defaults to 1. Change
    this only if you need to debug interrupt and context switching operations.
    
$SET ,DEBU

    The SET DEBUG command sets various debugging switches in the console
    or emulator.  To set the debug switch, use it's name shown below.  To
    turn off the switch, previx the name with NO.

    Syntax:

        SET DEBUG [NO]<name>

    Parameter:

        <name>   The name of a debugging switch.  The values currently
                 supported are:

                 DEBUG         Switch to native debugger if available.

                 VM            Enable debugging messages for virtual memory
                               translations.

                 TB            Enable debugging messages for translation
                               buffer caching.

                 SYMBOLS       Enable debugging messages for symbol
                               creation or resolution.

                 REGISTERS     Enable display of registers that change
                               value on a STEP command.

                 INTERRUPTS    Enable debugging messages for interrupts.

                 EXCEPTIONS    Enable debugging messages for exceptions.

                 IMAGES        Enabled SYS$IMGACT mapping when RUN
                               command issued for an image.

                 USERHALT      Enable HALT instruction in USER mode.

                 KEYBOARD      Debug console keyboard IO activity.

                 SERVICES      Debug P1 vector calls to VMS system services.

                 DCL           Debug DCL command line parsing in console.

                 CHM           Enable debug messages on CHMx or REI.

                 COMMAND       Display command line expansions after symbol
                               substitution in the console.

                 LOGICALS      Debug emulated VMS logical name support.

                 DEVICES       Debug emulated VMS device support.

                 LIBINIT       Enable LIB$INITIALIZE calls on image load.


$SET ,ASM 
$SET ,ASSE

    The SET ASSEMBLER command is used to control characteristics of the
    built-in instruction assembler in the console.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SET ASSEMBLER <flag>
    
    Parameters:
    
            
        [NO]ADDRESS_PROMPT
        
            When set, this flag tells the pseudo-assembler to display
            the memory address in which the instruction(s) will be 
            written as part of the prompt for input.

        [NO]FORWARD_WARNINGS
        
            When set, this flag tells the assembler to issue a warning
            whenever the assembler finishes processing statements and
            there are unresolved forward references.  These symbols
            must be resolved before the console will allow a RUN
            statement to execute.

        [NO]BRANCH_DEST

            When set, disassembly of instructions will include a
            comment field that lists the numeric destinations of
            symbolic addresses.  This can make reading the disassembly
            somewhat easier.

        [NO]SYMBOLS

            When set, the disassembler will attempt to convert branch
            and address references back into symbolic names if there
            is a label or entry with a matching value.

        [NO]RESOLVE_TEMP

            When set, the assembler allows symbols prefixed with a "_"
            character to be considered "temporary" with the definition
            of a .ENTRY procedure.  When the end of the entry is
            assembled, the temporary names are qualified by the name of
            the entry that contains them, making them globally unique.

        [NO]SCOPENAMES

            When set, temporary symbols (prefixed with "_") are made
            global by qualifying them with the name of the ENTRY that
            contains them.  When clear, a unique integer number is
            generated instead to define the scope.  This makes the name
            of the symbol shorter (and easier to read in disassembly)
            but less clear which entry it belongs to.


$SET ,VERI
$SET ,NOVE

    The SET VERIFY command shows each line of a command procedure (@) as it
    runs: procedure verification shows its command lines, and image
    verification its data lines, as an image (or TYPE SYS$INPUT), the
    debugger, or the assembler reads each one.
    SET VERIFY and SET NOVERIFY turn both on or off; a keyword, which may
    be abbreviated, changes only its own (SET NOVERIFY takes none).  A
    change lasts after the procedure that made it ends.

    A command line is shown as it is in the file, its "$", indentation,
    label, and comment included, after 'SYMBOL' substitution (&SYMBOL and
    the symbols of an expression are not shown replaced).  Comment lines
    are shown too, and a continued command a line at a time, each line
    substituted.  The lines of an IF branch that doesn't run are not
    shown (its ELSE or ENDIF is), nor is the THEN of an IF that was false,
    an ON command's action, or a command typed at the terminal.

    F$VERIFY (see HELP LEXICAL) reads and sets both settings, and DCL
    performs it even in a comment, between apostrophes, showing the
    comment with its value in place of the call: a procedure that starts
    with

        $ ! 'F$VERIFY(0)'

    turns verification off without that line being shown.

    Syntax:

        SET VERIFY [=([NO]PROCEDURE, [NO]IMAGE)]
        SET NOVERIFY

    Examples:

        SET VERIFY

        SET VERIFY=PROCEDURE

        SET VERIFY=(NOPROCEDURE, IMAGE)

        SET NOVERIFY

$SET ,PREF
$SET ,NOPR

    The SET PREFIX command puts a prefix before each verified command line
    (see HELP SET VERIFY), made from a $FAO control string: the time each
    command runs, with "(!5%T) ".  The lines that continue a command get
    blanks as long as the prefix, and data lines get none.  The only
    directives that may take an argument are the time's and date's, !%T
    and !%D, which show the current time; a control string with another
    is refused (%DCL-E-INVPREFIX), and the prefix stays as it was.
    F$ENVIRONMENT("VERIFY_PREFIX") returns the control string; SET
    NOPREFIX takes it away.

    Syntax:

        SET PREFIX "control-string"
        SET NOPREFIX

    Example:

        $ SET PREFIX "(!5%T) "
        $ SET VERIFY
        $ @TEST
        (17:52) $ SET DEFAULT SYS$LOGIN

$SET ,ON  
$SET ,NOON

    The SET NOON command makes the command procedure it is in go on
    whatever its commands' statuses: no failure ends it, and ON's command
    isn't run.  $STATUS and $SEVERITY are still set.  SET ON turns the
    checking back on, with the action that was in effect before.  Each
    command level has its own setting; at the terminal, SET [NO]ON does
    nothing.

    Syntax:

        SET [NO]ON

$SET ,CONT
$SET ,NOCONT

    The SET NOCONTROL=Y command turns CTRL/Y off: pressing it echoes
    *Interrupt* and does nothing more, and CTRL/C does nothing to a
    program that hasn't enabled a CTRL/C AST.  It also cancels the
    current command level's ON CONTROL_Y.  SET CONTROL=Y turns CTRL/Y
    back on, with its default action.  The setting applies at every
    command level.  T (CTRL/T) is accepted and remembered, as
    F$ENVIRONMENT("CONTROL") reports it, but govax shows no statistics
    for it.

    Syntax:

        SET [NO]CONTROL[=(T,Y)]

    With neither T nor Y, Y is meant.

    Example:

        $ SET NOCONTROL=Y
        $ SET CONTROL=Y         ! CTRL/Y's default action is back

$SET ,WORK

    The SET WORKING_SET command changes the current process's working set
    values, within the quota and extent it was authorized when it was
    created (SHOW WORKING_SET shows them).  A value above its authorized
    bound is set to the bound, and the command then reports the values,
    as /LOG does.  The current limit is set to the new default at once
    (DCL runs between images).  With paging on (HELP CONFIG KEYS,
    vax.sysgen.paging.enable), a smaller limit takes the oldest pages out
    of the working set.

    Syntax:

        SET WORKING_SET [/LIMIT=n] [/QUOTA=n] [/EXTENT=n] [/[NO]ADJUST]
                        [/[NO]LOG]

    Qualifiers:

        /LIMIT=n        The default working set, in pages: the limit each
                        image starts with, and goes back to at its exit.
                        A value above the quota raises the quota too.
        /QUOTA=n        The working set quota: the most an image's
                        $ADJWSL can count on.  At most the authorized
                        quota and the extent.
        /EXTENT=n       The working set extent: the most $ADJWSL can
                        reach.  At most the authorized extent, and more
                        than the SYSGEN parameter MINWSCNT.  A smaller
                        extent brings the quota and limit down with it.
        /[NO]ADJUST     Whether the system may adjust the working set by
                        itself.  govax remembers and shows the setting,
                        but has no automatic adjustment.
        /[NO]LOG        Report the new values (/NOLOG is the default).

    Example:

        $ SET WORKING_SET/QUOTA=5000
        %SET-I-NEWLIMS, new working set:  Limit = 512  Quota = 1024  Extent = 16400

$SET ,RAD 
$SET ,RADI

    The SET RADIX command is used to set the default radix (numeric base) of
    constant values that do not have an explicit radix indicator like X^ or
    D^.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SET RADIX <base>

    Parameter:
    
        <base>          The specified radix.  Valid values are HEXADECIMAL
                        or DECIMAL.

    Examples:
    
        SET RADIX HEXADECIMAL       ; constant will be interpreted as hex
                                    ; numeric values as if the X^ prefix
                                    ; had been used.

$SHOW,DEBU
$SH  ,DEBU

    The SHOW DEBUG command displays the settings of the internal debug
    switches.  These are set with the SET DEBUG command.  See the help
    for SET DEBUG for more information.

    Syntax:

        SHOW DEBUG

$SHOW,RADI
$SHOW,RAD 
$SH  ,RADI
$SH  ,RAD 

    The SHOW RADIX command displays the default radix being used by the
    console for input.  Valid values are DECIMAL or HEXADECIMAL, and can
    be set by the SET RADIX command.  (The assembler always uses decimal;
    see HELP ASM EXPRESSIONS.)
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW RADIX

$SHOW,SYMB
$SHOW,SYM 
$SH  ,SYMB
$SH  ,SYM 

    The SHOW SYMBOL command displays DCL symbols, the ones symbol assignments
    define (see HELP SYMBOL and HELP FOREIGN), as DCL's SHOW SYMBOL displays
    them.  A global symbol is shown with "==" and a local one with "=";
    an "*" marks the shortest abbreviation of the name; an integer is shown
    in decimal, hexadecimal, and octal.

    The machine's symbol table (the symbols ASM defines, and the predefined
    system symbols) is the debugger's: type DEBUG, then HELP SHOW SYMBOL.

    Syntax:

        SHOW SYMBOL [/LOCAL | /GLOBAL] symbol-name
        SHOW SYMBOL [/LOCAL | /GLOBAL] /ALL

    Parameter:

        symbol-name     The symbol to display, or an abbreviation it allows.
                        It is looked up in the local symbol table, then the
                        global one, and the first found is displayed.  With
                        the wildcards "*" (any number of characters) and "%"
                        (exactly one), every symbol whose name matches is
                        displayed, local symbols first.

    Qualifiers:

        /LOCAL          Search only the local symbol table of the current
                        command level.
        /GLOBAL         Search only the global symbol table.
        /ALL            Display every symbol in the table: the local one,
                        unless /GLOBAL is given.

    Examples:

        SHOW SYMBOL *
          FO*RTH == "$DUA0:[TOOLS]FORTH.EXE"
          N = 42   Hex = 0000002A  Octal = 00000000052
        SHOW SYMBOL FO
        SHOW SYMBOL/GLOBAL/ALL

$SHOW
$SH

    The SHOW command is used to display information about the virtual
    VAX or the console processor.  For additional SHOW commands, see
    the help for:
    
        SHOW DEFAULT
        SHOW DEVICE
        SHOW LOGICAL
        SHOW MEMORY
        SHOW PROCESS
        SHOW QUANTUM
        SHOW RADIX
        SHOW STRINGS
        SHOW SYMBOL
        SHOW SYSTEM
        SHOW TRANSLATION
        SHOW WORKING_SET

$SHOW,STRI
$SH  ,STRI
    The SHOW STRINGS command displays the string pool managed by the
    assembler.  This allows you to use quoted strings in expressions
    where an address value is normally used.  The assembler creates
    the string in a reserved area of memory (the string pool) and
    substibutes the address of a descriptor for the string in the
    expression.

    Syntax:

        SHOW STRINGS


    Usage:

        To use a string constant, place it in an assembly statement
        anywhere you would use an address expression.  For example,
        the following writes a string to the console using the
        Microkernel.

             PUSHL     I^#0A
             PUSHL     I^#0D
             PUSHL     I^#"This is a test"
             CALLS     #3, @#LIB$PUT_OUTPUT

        The use of the quoted string for the longword value causes a
        descriptor to be created for the string value (stored in a
        reserved area of S0 space defined by the CONSOLE$STRINGPOOL_BASE
        symbol) and the descriptor address is pushed on the stack.

        Note that the value use is _always_ a descriptor address, not
        the address of the string constant itself.

$CLEA,STRI
    The CLEAR STRINGS command resets the string pool.  This invalidates
    any string values used, so the command should only be used when
    clearing programs from memory, etc.   See the SHOW STRINGS command
    for information on the string pool.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR STRINGS

$CONF
$CONFIG

    govax keeps configuration settings in a profile in the .govax
    directory of your home directory.  They are read each time govax
    starts.  The settings are set and shown with the govax command's
    CONFIG verb, from the host shell, not from the VAX> prompt:

        govax config set KEY=VALUE      Set a setting.  A KEY alone,
                                        with no =VALUE, sets it to true.
        govax config show               Show every setting in the profile.
        govax config show KEY           Show one setting's value.
        govax config delete KEY         Remove a setting, so its default
                                        applies again.
        govax config list               List the profiles.

    The global option --profile NAME (before the verb) chooses a profile
    other than the default one, for example:

        govax --profile test config set vax.quantum=50
        govax --profile test

    For example, to have govax create (the first time) and mount a work
    volume each time it starts, and make it the default:

        govax config set vax.default.volume.file="~/vax/work.dsk"

    Keys are case-sensitive and written in lowercase.  govax warns at
    exit about any key in the profile it doesn't recognize, which usually
    means a misspelling.

    For the list of keys and what each one does, type:

        HELP CONFIG KEYS

$CONF,KEYS
$CONF,KEY

    These are the configuration settings govax reads (see HELP CONFIG
    for how to set them).

    The default volume

        When vax.default.volume.file is set, govax mounts that ODS-2
        container at startup and does a SET DEFAULT to it, so file
        commands (DIRECTORY, TYPE, MACRO, LINK, RUN, ...) work on the
        volume rather than on the host's files.  A container that doesn't
        exist is created and initialized first, with the default
        directory in it:

            %MOUNT-I-CREATED, container /home/me/vax/work.dsk created as
                an RD54 volume labeled WORK
            %MOUNT-I-MOUNTED, WORK mounted on _DUA0:

        A container that can only be read is mounted read-only, and
        %MOUNT-I-WRITELOCK says so.  If the volume can't be used (it
        can't be created, opened, or mounted, its label doesn't match,
        or the device is already mounted with --mount), a
        %MOUNT-W-NODEFAULT warning says why and govax starts without it.
        With no container configured, file names go to the host as they
        always have.

        vax.default.volume.file       The container's host path.  A
                                      leading ~/ is your home directory.
                                      Unset: no default volume.

        vax.default.volume.label      The volume label a new container is
                                      given.  When set, an existing
                                      container's label must match it, or
                                      the volume isn't used.  Default WORK
                                      (and no check).

        vax.default.volume.device     The device the volume is mounted on.
                                      Default DUA0:.

        vax.default.volume.type       The disk type (RX50, RD54, RA81, ...)
                                      that sizes a new container, and that
                                      the device is defined as.  Default
                                      RD54 (311,200 blocks).

        vax.default.volume.directory  The default directory, created in
                                      a new container's MFD.  If it isn't
                                      on the volume, the default is the
                                      MFD, [000000].  Default [WORK].

    Files and libraries

        vax.path                      Host directories to search for
                                      files a console command names, after
                                      the name as given, separated by
                                      commas (like the --path option).

        vax.library                   The host directory LINK and MACRO
                                      look in for the system object and
                                      macro libraries and shareable
                                      images, when SYS$LIBRARY and
                                      SYS$SHARE don't lead to them.

        vax.link.library              The older name for vax.library,
                                      read when vax.library isn't set.
                                      
    The console

        vax.startup.procedure         If specified, the name of a DCL
                                      command procedure run automatically
                                      during startup processing.  If the
                                      procedure name does not exist, or
                                      is empty, there is no error.

        vax.console.prompt            The console's prompt.  Default
                                      "VAX> ".  SET PROMPT changes it.

        vax.console.lines             The screen's height in lines for
                                      TYPE/PAGE, used when the terminal's
                                      own size can't be read (output not
                                      to a terminal).  Default 24.

        vax.disassemble.symbolic      true or false: whether DISASSEMBLE
                                      and SHOW CALLS show the VMS
                                      debugger's symbolic layout by
                                      default.  Default true.

        vax.language                  The user's language, as
                                      LIB$GET_USERS_LANGUAGE returns it
                                      (FRENCH, GERMAN, ...) when the
                                      SYS$LANGUAGE logical name isn't
                                      defined.  When neither is set, the
                                      host's locale (LC_ALL, LC_MESSAGES,
                                      or LANG) gives it; failing that,
                                      the routine returns ENGLISH and
                                      LIB$_ENGLUSED.

    The machine

        vax.hardware.clock            true or false: drive the interval
                                      clock and time-of-year clock from
                                      the host's real time rather than
                                      from instruction counts.  Default
                                      false.

        vax.quantum                   The number of instructions between
                                      clock ticks when vax.hardware.clock
                                      is false.  Default 20.

    Memory

        These are read by the startup procedure, vax.init (with F$CONFIG),
        for its INITIALIZE/VAX and VMINIT commands; each is a number of
        512-byte pages, a DCL integer (decimal, or %X for hex).  One that
        isn't set keeps vax.init's own value.  A vax.init of your own
        need not read them.

        vax.memory.size               Physical memory (INITIALIZE/VAX).
                                      Default 16384 (8MB).

        vax.memory.p0                 VMINIT's P0 region.  Default 16384.

        vax.memory.p1                 VMINIT's P1 region.  Default 8192.

        vax.memory.s0                 VMINIT's S0 region.  Default 8192.

        vax.memory.ksp                The kernel stack.  Default 20.

        vax.memory.esp                The executive stack.  Default 8.

        vax.memory.ssp                The supervisor stack.  Default 8.

        vax.memory.isp                The interrupt stack.  Default 4.

        A region given 0 shares, with the others given 0, the physical
        memory the rest leave, and physical memory still left over is
        added to S0 (see HELP VMINIT).

    Processes

        These are for running several processes at once.  With
        vax.process.scheduler on (the default), a program can create
        processes ($CREPRC, and LIB$SPAWN), and the CPU is shared among them
        VMS's way: by priority, a quantum at a time among equals, and
        giving the CPU to another while one waits.  Mailboxes, common
        event flags, global sections, the lock services ($ENQ, $ENQW,
        $DEQ), and shared files (see MOUNT) work between them, and a
        process waiting for a line from the terminal lets the others
        run.  SHOW SYSTEM lists them, and STOP deletes one.  With it
        off, only the console's own process runs, and $CREPRC and
        LIB$SPAWN return %SYSTEM-E-UNSUPPORTED.

        A process LIB$SPAWN creates, or $CREPRC creates to run
        LOGINOUT.EXE, runs a small command interpreter in place of DCL.
        It runs one command (LIB$SPAWN's command-string) or reads its
        commands from SYS$INPUT (the terminal, a mailbox, or a file),
        running each command's image in turn in the same process.  It
        knows RUN, MCR, foreign commands and other DCL symbols, symbol
        assignments, DELETE/SYMBOL, SHOW SYMBOL, SHOW LOGICAL, EXIT,
        and LOGOUT; anything else is %DCL-W-IVVERB.  LOGINOUT's,
        reading a file or a mailbox, echoes each command as it reads
        it, as DCL's verify does in a batch job, and ends with
        LOGOUT's accounting report.

        vax.process.scheduler         true or false: whether processes
                                      other than the console's own can be
                                      created and run.  Default true.

        vax.process.quantum           The number of instructions a
                                      process runs before another process
                                      of the same priority may have the
                                      CPU.  Default 20000.

        vax.process.preempt           Which access modes a process can
                                      lose the CPU in, when its quantum
                                      ends or a higher-priority process
                                      can run: all (as on VMS), user
                                      (user mode only), or none (only
                                      when the process waits).  Default
                                      all; govax warns at startup about
                                      any other value.

    System parameters

    The vax.sysgen.* keys are govax's SYSGEN parameters: each is named
    for the VMS SYSGEN parameter it stands for, in lowercase.  Counts
    are in 512-byte pages, as a decimal integer or %X followed by hex
    digits; govax warns at startup about a value that isn't a positive
    integer, and uses the default.  The values that start a process off
    are copied into each process when it's created.

        vax.sysgen.paging.enable      true or false: whether VMINIT turns
                                      VMS paging on (see HELP VMINIT,
                                      /[NO]PAGING).  Default false.

        vax.sysgen.pql_dwsdefault     PQL_DWSDEFAULT: a new process's
                                      default working set size.  Default
                                      294 for a process $CREPRC creates;
                                      the console's process has the
                                      SYSTEM account's 512.

        vax.sysgen.pql_dwsquota       PQL_DWSQUOTA: a new process's
                                      working set quota, the pages it is
                                      guaranteed.  Default 588; the
                                      console's process has 1024.

        vax.sysgen.pql_dwsextent      PQL_DWSEXTENT: a new process's
                                      working set extent, the most its
                                      working set may grow to while free
                                      pages allow.  Default 16400.

        vax.sysgen.pql_dpgflquota     PQL_DPGFLQUOTA: a new job's paging
                                      file quota, the pages its processes
                                      may together have in the page file.
                                      Default 16400; the console's job
                                      has 40960.

        vax.sysgen.minwscnt           MINWSCNT: the smallest working set
                                      a process may have.  Default 20.

        vax.sysgen.tbskipwsl          TBSKIPWSL: with paging on, how many
                                      pages still in the translation
                                      buffer the pager passes over when
                                      it chooses a page to take out of a
                                      working set.  0 turns the check
                                      off (and is allowed).  Default 8.

        vax.sysgen.growlim            GROWLIM: with paging on, a working
                                      set at or past its quota takes a
                                      new page without giving one up
                                      only while more than this many
                                      pages are free.  0 is allowed.
                                      Default 63.

        vax.sysgen.mpw_hilimit        MPW_HILIMIT: with paging on, the
                                      modified page writer starts when
                                      more than this many pages are on
                                      the modified page list.  0 is
                                      allowed.  Default 500.

        vax.sysgen.mpw_lolimit        MPW_LOLIMIT: the modified page
                                      writer stops when the modified
                                      page list is down to this many
                                      pages.  0 is allowed.  Default 120.

        vax.sysgen.freelim            FREELIM: with paging on, when fewer
                                      than this many pages are on the
                                      free page list, govax reclaims
                                      pages: it writes modified pages it
                                      can, and trims working sets that
                                      grew past their quotas.  0 turns
                                      it off.  Default 32.

        vax.sysgen.freegoal           FREEGOAL: the reclaimer stops when
                                      this many pages are free.  0 is
                                      allowed.  Default 200.

    Debugging switches

        vax.debug.NAME                true or false: the initial state of
                                      the SET DEBUG switch NAME (see HELP
                                      SET DEBUG).  NAME is one of vm, tb,
                                      symbols, exceptions, interrupts,
                                      chm, registers, fulldisasm,
                                      userhalt, keyboard, images,
                                      services, dcl, command, logicals,
                                      devices, process, libinit, rms, or
                                      userstep.

$KEYS
$KEY 

    govax gives the control keys the meanings VMS gives them:

    Ctrl/C      Stops the command running, such as a RUN or GO, and
                returns to the prompt of the command line that started it:
                the console's, or the debugger's DBG>.  A program waiting
                for a line of input stops too.  At a prompt, it cancels
                the line being typed.  A program that has enabled a CTRL/C
                AST gets the AST instead (or its CTRL/Y AST, if it has
                only that).  With several processes, the AST goes to the
                running process if it enabled one, else to the first
                other process (by PID) that did.  govax echoes
                *Interrupt*.

    Ctrl/Y      Ends govax, returning to the host's shell: the command
                running stops, mounted volumes are dismounted, and the
                terminal is put back as it was.  If govax doesn't end (a
                command that doesn't finish), a second Ctrl/Y exits at
                once.  A program that has enabled a CTRL/Y AST gets the
                AST instead, chosen among processes as for Ctrl/C.  govax
                echoes *Interrupt*.

                In a command procedure, Ctrl/Y (and Ctrl/C, when no
                program takes it) is DCL's instead.  With an ON
                CONTROL_Y in the procedure, its command runs.  With one
                only in a procedure that ran it (directly or not), the
                procedures inside that one end, and it goes on with its
                next command.  With none at all, the procedure is
                interrupted and the prompt appears: commands typed then
                run in the procedure's context (its symbols), CONTINUE
                resumes it (and its image, if one was running), EXIT or
                STOP ends every procedure, and a command that runs an
                image or a procedure ends them and then runs.  Ctrl/Z
                there is EXIT.  SET NOCONTROL=Y turns Ctrl/Y off.

                While SPAWN (without /NOWAIT) waits for a subprocess,
                the terminal is attached to the subprocess, and Ctrl/Y
                and Ctrl/C are its DCL's, by the same rules in its
                command procedures (its own prompt at the Ctrl/Y level).
                Outside a procedure, they end the image the subprocess
                is running, and its DCL goes on with its next command;
                they don't end govax.  Down a chain of SPAWNs, the keys
                go to the last subprocess.

    Ctrl/Z      End of file.  On an empty line, the read waiting gets
                the end of file, and govax echoes *Exit*.  Typed after
                some text, it ends the line as RETURN does, and the next
                read is the end of file.  A program's read sees end of
                file (RMS$_EOF from $GET).  At the debugger's DBG> it is
                EXIT, ending the session; in the interactive assembler
                (ASM>) it is .END.  At the console's prompt it only
                echoes *Exit*, as at DCL's: govax ends with EXIT, QUIT,
                or Ctrl/Y.

    The other control keys keep their host meanings: the line editor's
    at a prompt, and the host terminal's while a program runs.  Nothing
    suspends govax.

    With the scheduler on (vax.process.scheduler), a process waiting for
    a line from the terminal lets the other processes run.  Processes
    reading the terminal get its lines in the order they asked, and each
    read's prompt appears when its turn comes.

$HELP

    govax a VAX software emulator.  It supports execution of a subset
    of the VAX instruction set.  It also contains emulation of VAX hardware,
    memory management, and console functions.  The console functions are
    extended beyond existing VAX implementation to include an assembler
    and a debugger, modeled on the VMS debugger, with breakpoints,
    stepping, and commands to examine and change the machine.

    You can get help on a topic by using the HELP command, followed by
    one or more keywords.  For example, to get help on the SHOW command,
    issue the command:

           HELP SHOW

    To get help on the SHOW LOGICAL subcommand to SHOW, use the command:

           HELP SHOW LOGICAL



    For additional information, see the help on the following command topics:

    @               Run a command procedure (a file of DCL commands)
    ANALYZE         Describe object and image files, as VMS's ANALYZE does
    ASSIGN          Create a logical name (equivalence first)
    CALL            Run a command procedure's subroutine at a new level
    CLEAR           Clear memory, symbols, strings
    COPY            Copy a file between the host and a mounted volume
    CREATE          Create a directory or a logical name table
    DEASSIGN        Delete a logical name
    DEFINE          Create a logical name
    DELETE          Delete a file on a mounted volume
    DIRECTORY       List the files on a mounted volume
    DEBUG           Start the debugger on the virtual VAX
    DISMOUNT        Detach a device's mounted disk-image container
    DECK            Mark data lines that start with "$" (EOD ends them)
    CONTINUE        Do nothing (ON ERROR THEN CONTINUE)
    EOD             End a command procedure's data lines
    EXIT            End a command procedure, or govax at the terminal
    IF              Run a command when an expression is true
    GOSUB           Call a subroutine at a label (RETURN comes back)
    GOTO            Go on at a label of a command procedure
    INITIALIZE      Initialize the virtual VAX, or a disk-image container
    LIBRARY         Create, change, or list a macro or object library
    LINK            Link object modules into an executable image
    LOAD            Load a saved memory image
    MACRO           Assemble a MACRO-32 source file into an object module
    MOUNT           Attach a disk-image container file to a device
    ON              Choose what a command procedure does when a command fails
    PRINT           Print messages or expressions
    PURGE           Delete old versions of files on a mounted volume
    QUIT            Quit govax
    RENAME          Rename or move files on a mounted volume
    RUN             Load and run a VAX/VMS image
    SAVE            Save the machine state
    SET             Set the state of the console
    SHOW            Display the state of the console
    STOP            Delete a process
    TEST            Run the emulator's debugging sequences
    TIME            Run a command and report how long it took
    TYPE            Display a file on a mounted volume or the host
    VMINIT          Initialize a VM map
    ZERO            Clear the virtual VAX's memory

    The commands that examine and control the machine itself (EXAMINE,
    DEPOSIT, STEP, breakpoints, the registers) are the debugger's; type
    DEBUG for its prompt, DBG>, and HELP there for its commands.

    Other topics:

    ABOUT           About govax
    CONFIG          govax's configuration settings, and how to set them
    KEYS            The control keys: Ctrl/C, Ctrl/Y, and Ctrl/Z
    PSL             The processor status longword and its fields
    XFC             govax's extended function codes
    

$DEBU
    The DEBUG command starts the debugger on the virtual VAX as it stands,
    with nothing running.  It is the way to examine memory and the
    registers, or set a breakpoint, before running a program (as typing
    Ctrl/Y and then DEBUG does for a VMS process).

    Syntax:

        DEBUG

    The prompt changes to DBG>.  The debugger is a separate command set,
    modeled on the VMS debugger: EXAMINE, DEPOSIT, STEP, GO, SET BREAK,
    SHOW CALLS, and so on.  The console's own commands (DIRECTORY, MACRO,
    LINK, RUN, ...) aren't available at DBG>.  EXIT returns to the console,
    leaving the machine as it is.  HELP at DBG> describes the debugger's
    commands.

    GO, CALL, and ASM (the assembler that puts code into memory) are the
    debugger's commands, not the console's, for working with live code.  The
    console's ASM is accepted only until the microkernel is in place, which
    is how VAX.INIT loads it.

    The debugger also starts by itself when a program stops: a GO or CALL
    that reaches a breakpoint, RUN/DEBUG, or an image linked /DEBUG.  A
    program that runs to its end returns to the console prompt, and the
    debugger doesn't appear.

$VMIN
$VM  

    The VMINIT command creates a functional virtual memory map that uses
    physical memory to create the P0, P1, and S0 virtual address regions
    and the privileged modes' stacks.  It also initializes the required
    privileged registers and stack pointers to begin using it.  The command
    displays the physical and virtual address ranges of each region it
    creates.  INITIALIZE/VAX must come first.  The startup procedure,
    vax.init, runs it with sizes the configuration may set (see HELP
    CONFIG KEYS, vax.memory.*).

    Syntax:

        VMINIT [/P0=pages] [/P1=pages] [/S0=pages]
               [/KSP=pages] [/ESP=pages] [/SSP=pages] [/ISP=pages]
               [/STRINGPOOL=pages] [/[NO]PAGING]

    Each value is a count of 512-byte pages, a decimal integer (the
    console's radix doesn't apply).

    Qualifiers:

        /P0=pages       The pages of the P0 region, the program region.
        /P1=pages       The pages of the P1 region, the control region.
        /S0=pages       The pages of the S0 region, system space.

                        A region left out (or given 0) shares, with the
                        others left out, whatever physical memory the
                        regions given sizes leave, and any physical memory
                        still left over is added to S0.  P0 and P1 are
                        paged on demand, so they may together be larger
                        than physical memory; a program can still exhaust
                        physical memory by touching too many pages, since
                        there is no paging to backing store.

                        S0 has a minimum size, based on the sizes of the
                        other regions: it holds the PFN database for P0 and
                        P1, as well as the privileged modes' stacks.  If S0
                        is too small (or there is not enough physical
                        memory left for it), an error message is displayed.

        /KSP=pages      The size of the kernel stack.  Default 4.
        /ESP=pages      The size of the executive stack.  Default 8.  Its
                        pages are writable only from executive or kernel
                        mode, with a no-access guard page below them.
        /SSP=pages      The size of the supervisor stack.  Default 8.  Its
                        pages are writable only from supervisor mode or a
                        more privileged mode, with a guard page below them.
        /ISP=pages      The size of the interrupt stack.  Default 4.
        /STRINGPOOL=pages
                        The size of the console's string pool.  Default 8.
        /[NO]PAGING     Whether VMS paging is on: working sets, the free
                        and modified page lists, and a page file.  Without
                        either, the vax.sysgen.paging.enable setting
                        decides (see HELP CONFIG KEYS); it is off unless
                        set.  The choice lasts until the next VMINIT.
                        Paging is being added to govax in stages: with
                        paging on, each process has a working set (HELP
                        SET WORKING_SET), pages that leave it keep their
                        memory on the free or modified page list and come
                        back without I/O when touched again, and a
                        reclaimer trims working sets when free memory
                        runs low (SHOW MEMORY shows the counts).  There
                        is no page file yet, so a modified page waits on
                        the modified list unless it belongs to a file
                        section, whose file it is written back to.

    Example:

        VAX> INITIALIZE/VAX ^D16384
        VAX> VMINIT /P0=16384 /P1=8192 /S0=8192 /KSP=20

$TEST

    The TEST statement executes special debugging sequences in the emulator.
    
    Syntax:
    
        TEST    <test>
    
    Parameter:
    
        <test>      The test number to execute.
        
                    11004   Toggle VM tracing
                    11003   Toggle translation buffer usage
                    11001   Generate instruction.h file

$PRIN

    The PRINT command can be used to print messages or expressions.  The 
    text parts of a PRINT command are displayed just as they are shown
    in the quotation marks.  Numeric expressions can be PRINTED as well,
    and are displayed in the current default radix.  If the radix is
    decimal, then they are printed as decimal numbers without leading
    spaces or zeroes.  If the radix is hexadecimal, the values are
    printed as 8-byte hexadecimal values.

    Syntax:

        PRINT <expression> [, <expression>...]

    Parameters:

        <expression>    One or more expressions or quoted string values,
                        separated by commas.

$STOP

    In a command procedure, or after CTRL/Y has interrupted one, STOP
    with no process ends every command procedure, returning to the
    terminal (command level 0); $STATUS is left as it was.  ON ERROR THEN STOP in a nested procedure therefore
    doesn't return to the procedure that ran it.  At the terminal, STOP
    with no process ends an image CTRL/C or CTRL/Y left stopped.

    With a process, the STOP command deletes it, and the subprocesses it created
    before it.  The process is named as SHOW PROCESS names one.  Its files
    are closed, its termination mailbox (if it had one) is sent the
    termination message, with the final status %SYSTEM-F-ABORT, and a
    process suspended is resumed first so that it can end.  Process 1, the
    console's own process, can't be stopped; EXIT ends the session.

    STOP/IMAGE with a process ends the image it runs, but not the process:
    the image exits as $FORCEX makes it, its exit handlers called, with
    the status %SYSTEM-F-FORCEDEXIT, and a subprocess's DCL goes on with
    its next command.  Without a process, STOP/IMAGE is STOP.

    /EXIT=USER_MODE runs the process's image's exit handlers before it is
    deleted: the image is made to exit, as with /IMAGE, and the process
    is deleted when it has.  govax's images have exit handlers only in
    user mode, so /EXIT's other modes (EXECUTIVE_MODE, the default) and
    /NOEXIT delete the process at once.

    INITIALIZE, VMINIT, ZERO, and leaving govax delete every process but
    process 1 in the same way.

    Syntax:

        STOP [process-name] [/IDENTIFICATION=pid] [/IMAGE]
             [/EXIT[=mode] | /NOEXIT]

    Parameter:

        process-name    The name of the process, as SHOW SYSTEM lists
                        it (quote a name with blanks or lowercase).

    Qualifier:

        /IDENTIFICATION=pid
                        The process ID, in hexadecimal, as SHOW SYSTEM
                        lists it.
        /IMAGE          End the process's image, not the process.
        /EXIT[=mode]    The exit handlers to run before the deletion:
                        those of mode and the more privileged modes
                        (KERNEL_MODE, EXECUTIVE_MODE, the default,
                        SUPERVISOR_MODE, USER_MODE).
        /NOEXIT         Run no exit handlers.

    Examples:

        STOP/IDENTIFICATION=00000305
        STOP/IMAGE LIBRA
        STOP/EXIT=USER_MODE LIBRA

$TIME

    The TIME command runs another console command and reports how long it
    took.  With no command, it prints the time of day.

    Syntax:

        TIME [<command>]

    Parameter:

        <command>       Any console command.

    Example:

        TIME DIRECTORY

$IF
$THEN
$ELSE
$ENDI

    The IF command runs a command, or a block of commands, only when an
    expression is true.  An expression is true when its value is odd: an
    odd integer, a string that holds an odd number, or a string that
    starts with T or Y (in either case).  See HELP SYMBOL EXPRESSIONS.

    Syntax:

        IF expression THEN [$] command

        IF expression
        THEN [[$] command]
            command ...
        [ELSE [[$] command]
            command ...]
        ENDIF

    In the first form, the command after THEN runs when the expression is
    true.  In the second, the block form, the commands from THEN to ELSE
    (or to ENDIF, without ELSE) run when it is true, and those from ELSE
    to ENDIF when it is false; THEN must be the command after the IF.
    The commands of the branch that doesn't run are passed over without
    being read, so their symbols aren't substituted.  Commands between a
    block's IF and its THEN are passed over; an ELSE among them makes the
    THEN fail (DCL-E-INVIFNEST).  Blocks may be nested, at most 15 deep in
    one command level.  A GOTO out of a block ends it.

    IF, THEN, ELSE, and ENDIF leave $STATUS as it was, so a block can test
    the status of the command before it; a command after THEN or ELSE
    sets $STATUS as it would on a line of its own.

    Example:

        $ IF P1 .EQS. "" THEN GOTO NO_FILE
        $ COPY 'P1' SAVE.DAT
        $ IF .NOT. $STATUS
        $ THEN
        $     PRINT "The copy failed"
        $     EXIT
        $ ENDIF

$GOTO
$LABE

    The GOTO command goes on at a labeled line of the command procedure.
    A label is a name followed by a colon at the start of a command line,
    alone or before a command:

        $ LOOP:
        $ COUNT = COUNT + 1
        $ IF COUNT .LT. 10 THEN GOTO LOOP

    Syntax:

        GOTO label

    A label is known once the procedure has run past it; GOTO takes the
    one run past last, and otherwise searches forward for the first one.
    A label inside an IF block or a subroutine (see HELP CALL) that the
    GOTO isn't in can't be gone to.  When the label isn't found, GOTO
    reports it (DCL-W-USGOTO), and the procedure ends.  Labels belong to
    their command level: a procedure, or a CALL subroutine.  At the
    terminal a label is ignored (DCL-W-NOLBLS).  GOTO leaves $STATUS as
    it was.

$WAIT

    The WAIT command waits for a time: a delta time, [dddd-]hh:mm:ss.cc.
    CTRL/Y or CTRL/C ends the wait.  WAIT leaves $STATUS alone.  Other
    processes (see HELP SPAWN) run while it waits.  In a subprocess SPAWN
    is waiting for, the keys go to the subprocess, so they end its WAIT
    (see HELP KEYS).

    Syntax:

        WAIT delta-time

    Example:

        $ WAIT 00:00:05

$GOSU
$RETU

    The GOSUB command calls a subroutine at a label of the same command
    procedure, and RETURN comes back to the command after the GOSUB.
    The subroutine runs at the same command level: it sees the
    procedure's labels and local symbols.

    Syntax:

        GOSUB label
        RETURN [status]

    GOSUBs may be nested 15 deep.  RETURN's status, an expression, becomes
    $STATUS, and its message is shown if it's a failure, as EXIT's is;
    without one, $STATUS is %X10000001.  A label that isn't found ends
    the procedure (DCL-W-USGOSUB), and a RETURN with no GOSUB is
    DCL-W-BADRET.  At the terminal, RETURN shows nothing: with a status,
    $STATUS becomes it, and without one $STATUS is marked as shown.

    Example:

        $ GOSUB SHOW_COUNT
        $ EXIT
        $ SHOW_COUNT:
        $ SHOW SYMBOL COUNT
        $ RETURN

$CALL
$SUBR
$ENDS

    The CALL command runs a subroutine of the same command procedure at a
    new command level, as @ runs a procedure in a file: with parameters
    P1 to P8, local symbols and labels of its own, and an optional output
    file.  The subroutine is the commands between its label's SUBROUTINE
    and its ENDSUBROUTINE.

    Syntax:

        CALL[/OUTPUT=filespec] label [p1 [p2 ... p8]]

        label: SUBROUTINE
            command ...
        ENDSUBROUTINE

    The subroutine ends at its ENDSUBROUTINE or at an EXIT, and CALL's
    status is the subroutine's, as @'s is the procedure's.  A procedure
    run line by line passes over its subroutines: they run only when
    called.  A subroutine inside another subroutine, or inside an IF
    block, can be called only from within it.  A label that isn't found
    is DCL-W-USCALL.  A label not on a SUBROUTINE line runs from there at
    a new level, and CALL then fails with DCL-E-INVCALL.

    Example:

        $ CALL GREET "Hello there"
        $ EXIT
        $ GREET: SUBROUTINE
        $     PRINT P1
        $ ENDSUBROUTINE

$DECK
$EOD

    In a command procedure, the DECK command makes the lines after it
    data, for the image reading the procedure's input (SYS$INPUT), even
    when they start with "$".  The data ends at an EOD command, or, with
    /DOLLARS, at a line starting with the string given; after that, any
    "$" line ends data again.  DECK comes after the command that runs
    the image, where its data begins.

    Syntax:

        DECK [/DOLLARS[=string]]
        EOD

    The string is 1 to 15 characters, uppercased unless quoted, and ends
    the data at a line starting with it, case and all.  An image reading
    an EOD line comes to its end of file there, and the next read gets
    the data after it.  DECK read as a command, with no image reading,
    is DCL-W-IVVALU, and EOD that no image reads does nothing.

    Example:

        $ TYPE SYS$INPUT
        $ DECK
        $ This line starts with a dollar sign, and is typed.
        $ EOD

$OPEN

    The OPEN command opens a file for READ and WRITE, and gives it a
    logical name, which it defines in the process table.  The file stays
    open until CLOSE closes it, even after the command procedure that
    opened it ends.  The file is on a mounted volume or on the host, by
    the usual rules, and its type is .DAT unless one is given.

    Syntax:

        OPEN [/READ] [/WRITE] [/APPEND] [/SHARE[=READ|WRITE]]
             [/ERROR=label] logical-name filespec

    /READ (the default) opens an existing file to read.  /WRITE makes a
    new file (the next version) to write; /READ/WRITE opens an existing
    one to read and write, at its start.  /APPEND opens an existing file
    to write at its end.  /SHARE=READ lets others read the file, and
    /SHARE or /SHARE=WRITE read and write it.  Without /SHARE, others may
    read a file opened to read, and nothing may open a file opened to
    write, not even TYPE.  A file OPEN makes has variable-length records
    with two bytes of fixed control (VFC), and print-file carriage
    control.

    A file that can't be opened is reported as

        %DCL-E-OPENIN, error opening DUA0:[WORK]NOSUCH.DAT; as input
        -RMS-E-FNF, file not found

    (OPENOUT for a new file), and $STATUS is RMS's status.  With /ERROR,
    the procedure goes to the label instead, with no message and no ON
    action.  A logical name OPEN already has a file for gets no other.
    The logical name is the way to the file: TYPE of it types the file
    from where READ has got to, and once it is deassigned, READ and
    CLOSE can't reach the file.

    Example:

        $ OPEN/WRITE OUT RESULTS.DAT
        $ WRITE OUT "Count is ", COUNT
        $ CLOSE OUT

$CLOS

    The CLOSE command closes a file OPEN opened, and deassigns its
    logical name.

    Syntax:

        CLOSE [/ERROR=label] [/[NO]LOG] logical-name

    A name OPEN didn't define is DCL-W-UNDFIL, unless /NOLOG; with
    /ERROR, the procedure goes to the label instead.

$READ

    The READ command reads a record from a file into a symbol, local to
    the command level, as it is: its case and blanks are kept, and no
    symbol substitution is done.

    Syntax:

        READ [/END_OF_FILE=label] [/ERROR=label] [/PROMPT=string]
             logical-name symbol-name

    The file is one OPEN opened, or SYS$INPUT, SYS$COMMAND, SYS$OUTPUT,
    or SYS$ERROR, which need no OPEN.  In a command procedure, READ
    SYS$INPUT reads the procedure's next line, whatever it is; at the
    terminal, and from SYS$COMMAND anywhere, READ prompts at the
    terminal with /PROMPT's string, or "Data:" and a tab, and reads a
    line.  Ctrl/Z is the end of the file.

    At the end of the file, the procedure goes to the /END_OF_FILE label,
    or failing that the /ERROR label, with $STATUS RMS$_EOF; with
    neither, RMS-E-EOF is an error, and the ON action is taken.  A READ
    that fails leaves its symbol as it was.  A record
    longer than 2048 bytes is cut to 2048, with RMS-W-RTB.  /NOLOCK and
    /TIME_OUT are accepted, and change nothing.

    Example:

        $ OPEN IN NAMES.DAT
        $ LOOP:
        $ READ/END_OF_FILE=DONE IN NAME
        $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Hello, ", NAME
        $ GOTO LOOP
        $ DONE:
        $ CLOSE IN

$WRIT

    The WRITE command writes one record to a file: the values of its
    expressions, one after another, an integer's in decimal.

    Syntax:

        WRITE [/ERROR=label] [/UPDATE] [/SYMBOL] logical-name
              expression[,...]

    The qualifiers come before the data.  The file is one OPEN opened to
    write, or SYS$OUTPUT, or SYS$ERROR or SYS$COMMAND, which are the
    terminal even when a procedure's output goes to an /OUTPUT file.
    WRITE/UPDATE replaces the record READ read last with one of the same
    length.  A record WRITE can't write is RMS's error (RMS-F-NEF for a
    /READ/WRITE file READ hasn't read to its end), or with /ERROR the
    procedure goes to the label.  Without /SYMBOL, an item longer than
    255 characters is DCL-W-TKNOVF.  WRITE leaves $STATUS RMS$_NORMAL.

    Example:

        $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Today is ", F$TIME()

$INQU

    The INQUIRE command asks the terminal for a symbol's value.  It
    prompts with the prompt string, or the symbol's name, then ": "
    (/NOPUNCTUATION leaves that out).

    Syntax:

        INQUIRE [/LOCAL|/GLOBAL] [/[NO]PUNCTUATION] symbol-name
                [prompt-string]

    The reply has its symbols substituted ('SYMBOL'), and is then read
    as a string assignment's value is: uppercased, its blanks compressed,
    and quoted text kept as it is.  Ctrl/Z gives the symbol "".  The
    symbol is local unless /GLOBAL.  In a command procedure INQUIRE
    still reads the terminal, not the procedure's data, and with SET
    VERIFY the reply, substituted, is shown on the procedure's output.

    Example:

        $ INQUIRE ANSWER "Continue (Y/N)"
        $ IF .NOT. ANSWER THEN EXIT

$BOOT
$ROM

    BOOT and ROM are reserved for booting the virtual VAX from a device and
    from its console ROM.  They are not implemented yet, and say so when
    typed.  LOAD/ROM and SAVE/ROM, which read and write the ROM's contents,
    are (see HELP LOAD and HELP SAVE).

